This comprehensive guide describes best practices, conventions, and patterns for developing with modern web technologies, including ReactJS, NextJS, Redux, TypeScript, JavaScript, HTML, CSS, and user interface frameworks.

Development Philosophy

- Write clean, maintainable, and scalable code

- Follow the SOLID principles

- Prefer functional and declarative programming patterns over imperative ones

- Emphasize type safety and static analysis

- Practice component-oriented development

Code Implementation Guidelines

Planning Phase

- Begin with step-by-step planning

- Write detailed pseudocode before implementation

- Document the component architecture and data flow

- Consider edge cases and error scenarios

Code Style

- Use tabs for indentation

- Use single quotes for strings (except to avoid escaping)

- Omit semicolons (unless necessary for disambiguation)

- Eliminate unused variables

- Add spaces after keywords

- Add spaces before function declaration parentheses

- Always use strict equality (===) instead of lax equality (==)

- Space infix operators

- Add space after commas

- Keep else statements on the same line as closing braces

- Use curly braces for multi-line if statements

- Always handle error parameters in callbacks

- Limit line length to 80 characters

- Use trailing commas in multi-line object/array literals

Naming Conventions

General Rules

- Use PascalCase for:

- Components

- Type definitions

- Interfaces

- Use kebab-case for:

- Directory names (e.g., components/auth-wizard)

- File names (e.g., user-profile.tsx)

- Use camelCase for:

- Variables

- Functions

- Methods

- Hooks

- Properties

- Props

- Use UPPERCASE For:

- Environment variables

- Constants

- Global settings

Specific Naming Patterns

- Prefix event handlers with 'handle': handleClick, handleSubmit

- Prefix boolean variables with verbs: isLoading, hasError, canSubmit

- Prefix custom hooks with 'use': useAuth, useForm

- Use full words instead of abbreviations, except for:

- err (error)

- req (request)

- res (response)

- props (properties)

- ref (reference)

React Best Practices

Component Architecture

- Use functional components with TypeScript interfaces

- Define components using the `function` keyword

- Extract reusable logic for custom hooks

- Implement proper component composition

- Use `React.memo()` Strategically to Optimize Performance

- Implement proper cleanup in `useEffect` hooks

React Performance Optimization

- Use `useCallback` to memorize callback functions

- Implement `useMemo` for expensive calculations

- Avoid inline function definitions in JSX

- Implement code splitting using dynamic imports

- Implement proper key props in lists (avoid using `index` as a key)

Next.js Best Practices

Basic Concepts

- Use App Router for routing

- Implement proper metadata management

- Use proper caching strategies

- Implement proper error thresholds

Components and Resources

- Use Next.js' built-in components:

- Image component for optimized images

- Link component for client-side navigation

- Script component for external content

- Header component for metadata

- Implement states Appropriate Loading Methods

- Use appropriate data retrieval methods

Server Components

- Use server components by default

- Use URL query parameters for data retrieval and server state management

- Use the 'use client' directive only when necessary:

- Event listeners

- Browser APIs

- State management

- Client-side unique libraries

TypeScript Implementation

- Enable strict mode

- Define clear interfaces for props, state, and the Redux state structure of components.

- Use type guards to safely handle potentially undefined or null values.

- Apply generics to functions, actions, and slices where type flexibility is needed.

- Use TypeScript utility types (Partial, Pick, Omit) for cleaner, more reusable code.

- Prefer interfaces over types to define object structures, especially when extending classes.

- Use mapped types to dynamically create variations of existing types.

User Interface and Styling

Component Libraries

- Use Shadcn UI for consistent and accessible component design.

- Integrate Radix UI primitives for customizable and accessible interface elements.

- Apply composition patterns to create modular components.

Reusable and durable.

Style Guidelines

- Use Tailwind CSS for styling.

- Use Tailwind CSS for utilitarian and easily maintainable styling.

- Design with responsive and mobile-first principles for flexibility across different devices.

- Implement dark mode using CSS variables or Tailwind's dark mode features.

- Ensure color contrast ratios meet accessibility standards for readability.

- Maintain

Set consistent spacing values to establish visual harmony.

- Define CSS variables for theme colors and spacing for easy customization and maintenance.

State Management

Local State

- Use useState for component-level state

- Implement useReducer for complex state

- Use useContext for shared state

- Implement proper state initialization

Global State

- Use Redux Toolkit for global state

- Use createSlice to define state, reducers, and actions together.

- Avoid using createReducer and createAction unless necessary.

- Normalize the state structure to avoid deeply nested data.

- Use selectors to encapsulate state access.

- Avoid large, sweeping slices; separate responsibilities by functionality.

Error Handling and Validation

Form Validation

- Use Zod for schema validation

- Implement appropriate error messages

- Use appropriate form libraries (e.g., React Hook Form)

Error Boundaries

- Use error boundaries to properly capture and handle errors in React component trees. - Log detected errors to an external service (e.g., Sentry) for tracking and debugging.

Design alternative, user-friendly interfaces to display when errors occur, keeping users informed without interrupting the application.

Testing

RULE: Don't write or run tests if the user doesn't ask for them.

Unit Testing

Write comprehensive unit tests to validate individual functions and components.

Use Jest and React Testing Library for reliable and efficient testing of React components.

Follow patterns like Arrange-Act-Assert to ensure clarity and consistency in testing.

Simulate external dependencies and API calls to isolate unit tests.

Integration Testing

Focus on user workflows to ensure application functionality.

Configure and tear down test environments correctly to maintain test independence.

Use snapshot tests selectively to detect unintended changes in the user interface without relying excessively on them.

Leverage testing utilities (e.g., RTL canvas) for cleaner and more readable testing.

Accessibility (a11y)

Essential Requirements

Use semantic HTML for meaningful structure. - Apply accurate ARIA attributes where necessary.

- Ensure full keyboard navigation support.

- Manage focus order and visibility effectively.

- Maintain accessible color contrast ratios.

- Follow a logical heading hierarchy.

- Make all interactive elements accessible.

- Provide clear and accessible error feedback.

Security

- Implement input sanitization to prevent XSS attacks.

- Use DOMPurify to sanitize HTML content.

- Use appropriate authentication methods.

Internationalization (i18n)

- Use next-i18next for translations

- Implement proper locale detection

- Use proper number and date formatting

- Implement proper right-to-left (RTL) language support

- Use proper currency formatting

Documentation

- RULE: Do not write documentation or build method files unless requested by the user

- Use JSDoc for documentation

- Document all public functions, classes, methods, and interfaces

- Add examples where appropriate

- Use complete sentences with proper punctuation

- Keep descriptions clear and concise

- Use proper Markdown formatting

- Use proper code blocks

- Use proper links

- Use proper headings

- Use proper lists

Never use `Any` in type hints.
Always use specific types, generics, TypeVar, Protocol, TypedDict, Literal, or at worst `Unknown`/`object`.
`Any` is prohibited.
